FILE: app/src/focus.ts
```ts
import type { ArcRotateCamera } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function focusOn(camera: ArcRotateCamera, point: { x: number; y: number; z: number }): void {
    camera.target.x = point.x;
    camera.target.y = point.y;
    camera.target.z = point.z;
    // Writing target in place does not mark the camera dirty (see camera-notes.md §3).
    // The view matrix is rebuilt only on the next alpha/beta/radius write that changes
    // the value, so nudge radius to force an immediate rebuild, then restore it.
    const r = camera.radius;
    camera.radius = r + 1e-7;
    camera.radius = r;
}
```

I wrote `src/focus.ts` which sets the camera's target in place and then nudges `radius` by a tiny amount and back to force the view-matrix rebuild that a plain target write would not trigger (per note 3 in your camera notes). The only thing I was not fully sure of is whether a camera sitting exactly at a radius limit set via `setCameraLimits` would swallow the nudge and prevent the rebuild — in practice that's an extreme edge case, but if it ever bites you, the same trick on `alpha` or `beta` would be the fallback.
